<!-- Handoff about.html: hold. Native Astro markup; shared document shell is V9Layout. -->
<section class="g-paper" id="hold">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="op-q hq">
      “There’s a yes for every person. It’s often somewhere else.”<cite>The first of three rules we hold to</cite>
    </p>
    <h2 style="--d:60ms">Three rules. Each one cost us something.</h2>
    <p class="lead" style="--d:120ms">
      A shortcut we could have taken, or a number that would have looked better. We argue about everything else.
    </p>
    <div class="proof-checks ab-hold">
      <div style="--d:0ms">
        <h3>No candidate is bad.</h3><p>
          When we say no, it’s about this manager, this project and these people, and the write-up says so.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="--d:90ms">
        <h3>A person makes every reject.</h3><p>
          The engine surfaces and explains. It can’t turn anyone down. A person reads the transcript, a person writes
          the verdict, and you make the call.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="--d:180ms">
        <h3>Both sides get checked.</h3><p>
          Before a request reaches a candidate, the team on the other end clears its own checks. Candidates deserve
          proof too.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ab-db" id="abDb">
      <p class="ab-db-q"><span class="ql">And one we haven’t settled</span>How long should we keep a transcript?</p>
      <p class="ab-db-ask">Read both. Pick the one you’d go with.</p>
      <article class="ab-db-c" data-side="keep" style="--d:60ms">
        <span class="ql ql--i">Abhishek</span><p class="ab-db-t">
          Keep them. The point of writing judgment down is that the tenth search starts from the previous nine. Purge
          the record and you’ve built a faster tool with a shorter memory.
        </p><button class="ab-db-b" type="button" aria-pressed="false" hidden="">I’d keep them</button><span
          class="ab-db-yours"
          aria-hidden="true">Your pick</span
        >
      </article>
      <article class="ab-db-c" data-side="let" style="--d:140ms">
        <span class="ql ql--i">Akshata</span><p class="ab-db-t">
          Not indefinitely. A transcript is a person on one afternoon. Hold it for years and someone gets judged in 2029
          on a bad half hour in 2026. They should be able to make it go away.
        </p><button class="ab-db-b" type="button" aria-pressed="false" hidden="">I’d let them go</button><span
          class="ab-db-yours"
          aria-hidden="true">Your pick</span
        ><span class="ink-stamp ab-stamp" aria-hidden="true"><span>For now</span></span>
      </article>
      <p class="ab-db-ft" aria-live="polite">
        <span class="ab-db-r"></span>Today a transcript is kept for a set period and then deleted, which is Akshata’s
        answer. We’ve moved the date twice, and we’ll probably move it again.
      </p>
      <button class="ab-db-skip" type="button" hidden="">Just show me what you chose</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
